		{"id":"e58dc1831a691ab5","type":"file","file":"Pasted image 20260908110450.png","x":-460,"y":-1000,"width":560,"height":604},
		{"id":"4a6bc49a56caf02d","type":"text","text":"# **Income Statement/ Profit and Loss**\n\nDefines profit or loss generated by a company during a given time period.\n\n* Revenues: the amount charged for delivery of products or services.\n* Expenses: the cost of using up company resources to earn revenues.\n\nProfit(loss) = Revenues - Expenses","x":-1200,"y":-1200,"width":660,"height":260},
		{"id":"b5e74bd71904cb1b","type":"text","text":"# **Kinds of Profit**\n\n### Gross profit\n\n* Revenues - Cost of sales\n(cost of sales not only cost incurred by a company)\n\n### Operating profit/income (EBIT)\n\nOperating income = Gross profit - other operating expenses\n\n(other operating expenses like marketing, utility costs, admin depreciation expenses etc.)\n\n*EBITDA is operating income before interest, taxes, depreciation and amortisation*\n\nEBITDA = EBIT (or operating income) + Depreciation + Amortisation\n\n### Net Profit\n\nIf the company has borrowed money to finance its activities, it will have to pay interest. Deducting interest expense from operating income determines a companies profit before tax:\n\nEarnings before taxes = EBIT (or operating profit) - Interest expense\n\nIncome taxes owed by company on its earnings are then deducted to arrive at net income/profit or profit after tax:\n\nNet income = EBIT - Interest expense - tax expense.\n\nCompany shareholders are interested to know about income per share, called earnings per share (EPS) which is net income divided by number of shares outstanding and dividends per share.\n\nEBIT: Earnings before interest and taxes\nEBITDA: Earnings before interest, taxes, depreciation and amortisation\n\n\n","x":-1200,"y":-880,"width":660,"height":260}
	],
	"edges":[]
}